poetry:l10-y12@claude-sonnet-5-5.text:
The kettle hums its song before the house is up,
a thin blue thread of steam unwinding over the stove;
quietly, I hold the morning like a borrowed cup
and drink it slowly, sip by sip, careful, as with love,
and nothing waits to claim it from the rooms above.

I sit, and find there's nothing else I could desire;
outside, the quiet street has not yet learned its noise,
the crows rehearse their quarrel on the sagging wire;
no hurry anywhere, no call, no other voice,
just the light that climbs the wall, a little higher.

poetry:l10-y10@claude-sonnet-5-5.text:
The kettle hums before the house is up,
a thin blue thread of steam above the stove;
I hold the morning like a borrowed cup
and drink it slowly, careful, as with love.
The tea is slow to darken, and I wait;
the hour is mine, and nothing makes it late.

Outside, the street has not yet learned its noise,
the crows rehearse their quarrel on the wire;
there is no hurry here, no other voice,
just light that climbs the wall a little higher.

