poetry:l12-y13@claude-opus-5-5.text:
The kettle hums its quiet note before the house is up,
a thin blue thread of steam that curls above the waiting stove;
I hold the early morning gently, like a borrowed cup,
and drink it slowly, carefully, in sips, as if with love.

Outside, the street still sleeps and has not yet learned its noise,
the windowpane turns softly pale against the thinning grey;
no hurry here, no list to keep, and not one other voice,
and nothing yet is asked of me by all the coming day.

I wait, and let the kettle take as long as it will take;
down the road, the crows rehearse their quarrel on the wire;
the house is mine a little while, and no one is awake,
just light that climbs the kitchen wall a little higher.

poetry:l10-y10@claude-fable-5-2.text:
The kettle hums before the house is up,
a thin blue thread of steam above the stove;
I hold the morning like a borrowed cup
and drink it slowly, careful, as with love.

The warmth comes through the china to my hands;
nothing is asked; the pot steeps where it stands.

Outside, the street has not yet learned its noise,
the crows rehearse their quarrel on the wire;
there is no hurry here, no other voice,
just light that climbs the wall a little higher.

poetry:l10-y10@gpt-5.6-luna.text:
The kettle hums before the house is up,  
A thin blue thread of steam above the stove;  
We hold one unhurried hour like a cup,  
While nothing gets crossed off, and that’s the point:  
The rush feels like progress; still, we move slowly.  

Outside, the street has not yet learned its noise,  
The crows rehearse their quarrel on the wire;  
The day finds a steadier measure and voice;  
For one week, let the kettle take its time;  
We are better company for work that waits.

poetry:l6-y10@gpt-5.6-luna.text:
The kettle hums before the house wakes up,  
a thin blue thread of steam above the stove;  
I hold one hour like a borrowed cup.  

For one week, let the kettle take its time;  
the work will wait; light climbs the wall higher;  
we meet the day as better company.

